![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

The `ReadableByteChannel.read(ByteBuffer dst)` method is not guaranteed to read the entire requested length in a single invocation. It may read any number of bytes (at least one, if blocking) and return. In high-concurrency or network-heavy environments (like GCS), a single `read` call frequently returns fewer bytes than requested even if the end of the stream has not been reached. This will cause `numberOfBytesRead` to be less than `length`, resulting in a premature `EOFException`.

To ensure correctness and robustness, we should read in a loop until the requested `bytesToRead` has been fully read or EOF is encountered. Note that if `read` returns 0, we should continue the loop rather than breaking, as some implementations may return 0 bytes occasionally without indicating an infinite loop or EOF.

```java
                int numberOfBytesRead = 0;
                while (numberOfBytesRead < bytesToRead) {
                  int read = byteChannel.read(
                      ByteBuffer.wrap(buffer, offset + numberOfBytesRead, bytesToRead - numberOfBytesRead));
                  if (read < 0) {
                    break;
                  }
                  numberOfBytesRead += read;
                }
```

